==English==

===Etymology===
Late 14th century, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|posterité}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|posteritas}}, from {{m|la|posterus||following, coming after}}, from {{m|la|post||after}} ({{cog|en|post-}}).<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

Displaced Old English words such as {{m|ang|æftergengnes}}, {{m|ang|æfterweardnes}}, and {{m|ang|cnēores}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/pɒˈstɛɹɪti/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-posterity.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪti}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛɹəti}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|posterities}}

# All the [[future]] [[generation]]s, especially the [[descendant]]s of a [[specific]] person.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}
|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=The original family who had begun to build a palace to rival Nonesuch had died out before they had put up little more than the gateway, so that the actual structure which had come down to '''posterity''' retained the secret magic of a promise rather than the overpowering splendour of a great architectural achievement.}}

